course: history
lesson: 27
title: Explorers: Magellan around the world, and the people already there
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: In 1519 Ferdinand Magellan, a Portuguese sailor working for Spain, set off west with five ships and about 270 men to reach the Spice Islands of Asia.
explain: He found a stormy passage at the tip of South America and came out onto an ocean so calm that he named it the Pacific, meaning peaceful, never guessing it was the biggest ocean on Earth.
explain: Months later, starving and sick, the crew reached the Philippines, where Magellan was killed fighting the local leader Lapulapu, so Magellan himself never sailed all the way round.
explain: In 1522 one battered ship, the Victoria, came home with 18 men, and they found their diaries were one day behind, like a lap of a race where you lose a sunrise by running the same way the Sun goes.
explain: Everywhere these explorers went, people already lived, with names for every river and mountain, so the word discovered tells only the Europeans' side.
fact: In 1519 Ferdinand Magellan, a Portuguese sailor working for Spain, left with five ships and about 270 men to reach Asia by sailing west.
fact: Magellan found a stormy passage at the tip of South America, now called the Strait of Magellan, and entered an ocean he named the Pacific, meaning peaceful.
fact: Crossing the Pacific took months; sailors ate sawdust and leather, and many died of scurvy, an illness caused by a lack of fresh fruit and vegetables.
fact: Later sailors learned that lemons and limes prevent scurvy, because they contain vitamin C.
fact: In 1521 Magellan was killed in the Philippines in a battle with Lapulapu, who defended his island and is now a national hero there.
fact: In 1522 only one ship, the Victoria, with 18 men led by Juan Sebastián Elcano, returned to Spain after the first voyage around the world; so few men could hardly handle a ship built for a much bigger crew.
fact: The voyage proved that ships could sail all the way around the Earth, and that the Earth was bigger than people had thought.
fact: When the Victoria returned, the crew's diaries were one day behind the calendar, because sailing west around the world they had seen one sunrise fewer.
fact: Spanish soldiers called conquistadors conquered huge lands in the Americas looking for gold, and native peoples lost their land and freedom.
fact: Saying Europeans discovered a land forgets the people who had lived there for thousands of years.
fact: John Cabot reached Newfoundland in 1497 and Jacques Cartier sailed up the St Lawrence River in the 1530s, both looking for a northern passage to Asia.
fact: The name Canada comes from kanata, an Iroquoian word for village that Cartier heard.
fact: Old maps had sea monsters and blank spaces where mapmakers did not know what was there, and within about a hundred years European maps showed fairly accurate coastlines.
exercise: How many ships did Magellan start with? | Five | Only one | Fifty | Three
exercise: Which country paid for Magellan's voyage? | Spain | Portugal | England | China in Asia
exercise: What does Pacific mean? | Peaceful | Very stormy | Enormously big | Very salty
exercise: Where is the Strait of Magellan? | At the tip of South America | Between England and France, in the sea | In the middle of Africa | Next to Japan
exercise: What illness killed many sailors crossing the Pacific? | Scurvy | The Black Death | Chickenpox | Malaria from penguins
exercise: What stops scurvy? | Fresh fruit like lemons and limes | Salted meat and hard ship's biscuits | Sea water | Sleeping more
exercise: Where was Magellan killed? | In the Philippines | In Brazil, in South America | In Spain | In India
exercise: Who is a hero in the Philippines for resisting Magellan? | Lapulapu | Elcano, his sailor | Christopher Columbus | Jacques Cartier
exercise: Who brought the ship home after the voyage around the world? | Juan Sebastián Elcano | Ferdinand Magellan himself | Vasco da Gama | James Cook
exercise: How many men came home on the Victoria? | 18 | 270 | 2 | 180
exercise: Why had the sailors lost a day? | Sailing west around the world they saw one sunrise fewer | They slept for a whole day | Someone tore a page out of the ship's diary by mistake in a storm | The calendar in Spain was wrong
exercise: What were the conquistadors looking for? | Gold | Spices only | Ice for drinks | Kangaroos
exercise: Why is the word discovered tricky? | People already lived in those lands | Nobody ever went anywhere far from home | It is a French word | Maps did not exist
exercise: Where does the name Canada come from? | Kanata, an Iroquoian word for village | A Spanish word meaning there is no gold here | Cartier's ship | A French king
exercise: What were Cabot and Cartier looking for? | A northern passage to Asia | The South Pole | The lost city of Atlantis | Gold in Africa
exercise: Why did old maps have sea monsters? | Mapmakers filled spaces they knew nothing about | Monsters were seen by sailors every day in those seas | Sailors drew them for fun only | They marked good fishing spots
test: Magellan = first guy round the world, done. agree? | No, he was killed on the way; his crew finished it | Yes, he sailed all the way home and was given a castle | Yes, and he did it twice | No, Columbus went round first
test: You fly round the world westward and keep a diary without ever changing it. What do you find at home? | Your diary is one day behind | Your diary is one week ahead | Nothing is different | You have lost a year
test: If Magellan's sailors had had lots of lemons on board, what would probably have changed? | Far fewer would have died of scurvy | They would have sailed backwards | They would have reached Asia in a day | Nothing at all
test: Why would a ship of only 18 men struggle to sail home? | A big ship needs many hands to sail it | Small crews were forbidden by the king of Spain | 18 men ate too much food | Ships sailed better empty
test: Your friend's game says 'Discover Philippines'. What would Lapulapu say? | His people already lived there and knew it well | Thank you for finding us | We were waiting for you to come and give us a name | Nothing, nobody lived there
test: Why did Magellan think the Pacific would be a short crossing? | Nobody in Europe knew how huge it was | He had a perfect map of it | He thought it was a lake | He planned to swim
test: proper rough voyage innit, what did they even eat at the end | Sawdust and leather, when the food ran out | Fresh meat and lemons from every island they passed | Fresh pizza | Only chocolate
test: Why did the voyage prove the Earth was bigger than people thought? | The Pacific alone took months to cross | They measured it with a rope | They saw the Earth from space | The ships got smaller
test: Hockey fans in Toronto shout Canada. What were they actually shouting, long ago? | An Iroquoian word for village | A Spanish word for sea | A French word for cold | An English word for beaver
test: A map has a sea monster drawn in the middle of an ocean. What does it most likely mean? | The mapmaker did not know what was there | A real monster lived there | There was an island shaped like a monster | It was a famous restaurant
exam: Identify the individual under whose command the vessel Victoria completed the first circumnavigation of the globe. | Juan Sebastián Elcano | Ferdinand Magellan | Christopher Columbus | Jacques Cartier
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the fate of Ferdinand Magellan? | He was slain in the Philippines in an engagement with Lapulapu | He returned to Spain in triumph | He perished in the Strait that bears his name | He settled in Canada
exam: Ascertain the cause of the discrepancy of one day observed in the records of the returning crew. | Their westward circumnavigation entailed one fewer sunrise | An error in the calendar of Spain | The loss of a page of the ship's log | A day spent in harbour unrecorded
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the etymology of the name Canada. | It derives from kanata, an Iroquoian word for village | It derives from the Spanish for nothing here, said by early sailors | It derives from the name of a French ship | It derives from a Latin word for cold
